1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,380
Hi, we are Team BTYECODE, and this

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,680
is Paradox, an idea for the security

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:08,170
track of VoyageHack.

4
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:10,480
Here's the thing about B2B travel.

5
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:11,980
The bugs that cost real money

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,540
don't look like bugs.

7
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:15,740
A refund that pays out twice?

8
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:18,280
The server says 200 OK.

9
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:20,460
Same with a fare the agent sets

10
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:23,080
themselves, or one agency reading

11
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,320
another agency's passports.

12
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:27,100
Scanners look for bad inputs.

13
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,320
We look for outcomes that

14
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:30,920
should be impossible.

15
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:34,820
Paradox starts with just what TBO gives us:

16
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:37,660
a staging link, a few test accounts,

17
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:39,740
some recorded traffic and logs.

18
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,160
No API docs.

19
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:44,000
From that, it works out the routes, how

20
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:46,680
people actually book, who owns what, and

21
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:48,000
where the money sits.

22
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:51,900
Then it plays with those journeys the

23
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:53,220
way a sneaky user would.

24
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,920
Cancel twice.

25
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:55,880
Skip the payment.

26
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:57,040
Set your own fare.

27
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:58,520
Pretend to be another agency.

28
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,840
After each try, it checks 12 simple rules.

29
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:04,180
Your wallet can't end up richer.

30
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,180
The same booking can't get cheaper.

31
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:08,820
One agency never sees another's data.

32
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:10,760
If a rule breaks, that's a finding.

33
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:12,480
If not, it stays quiet.

34
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:14,500
Like this one.

35
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:15,900
Cancel a booking that's already

36
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:21,660
cancelled, and the wallet gains ₹7,776.

37
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:22,820
Every single time.

38
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:26,560
Paradox ran it 3 times to be sure, and

39
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:28,880
cut it down to just 4 requests.

40
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,293
Every booking it creates carries fake details:

41
00:01:32,313 --> 00:01:34,980
a made-up name, email and passport.

42
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,980
If those turn up in another agency's

43
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,080
account, the leak proves itself.

44
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:41,760
Each finding turns into a ticket for

45
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:44,100
the team that owns that code, with what

46
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:46,220
it costs in rupees, the exact steps,

47
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:49,460
and how to fix it.

48
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:51,220
And it keeps watching.

49
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:53,240
When a new release went out, Paradox

50
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:57,120
tested the changed part first.

51
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:58,860
The new amend feature trusted

52
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,160
whatever total the client sent.

53
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:02,673
It was flagged as critical

54
00:02:02,693 --> 00:02:04,040
about 12 seconds later.

55
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:07,240
Once the fix shipped, Paradox re-ran

56
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:08,980
its proofs, and the fixed tickets

57
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,560
closed on their own.

58
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,480
Along the way, it maps every endpoint, even

59
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,180
an old v1 route nobody remembered, which

60
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:18,580
was still leaking passports.

61
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:19,860
Does it work?

62
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:22,600
We planted 172 random bugs.

63
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,280
Paradox found all of them, with

64
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:25,620
zero false alarms.

65
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:29,980
A regular scanner found about 30% of the bugs.

66
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,960
We also tried it on an airline

67
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,540
platform it had never seen.

68
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:37,100
After two small, general fixes, it

69
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,400
caught all 7 planted bugs, and stayed

70
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,760
silent on the clean version.

71
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:43,680
And it's careful.

72
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:46,600
It stays in scope, only touches its own

73
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:49,433
test bookings, slows down if the server

74
00:02:49,453 --> 00:02:51,460
struggles, has a kill switch, and logs

75
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:52,740
every request.

76
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,080
That's Paradox.

77
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,800
It catches the bug that makes money

78
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:59,020
appear, before someone else does.

79
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:00,060
Thank you.
